This is a greedy simulation, not an optimal solver.

**Approach.** Keep each contributor's current skill levels and the day they become free. Repeatedly pick the project that can start earliest and, among those, has the best value. Assign contributors to its roles, run it, and update the levels.

**Project selection.** Keep a list of unscheduled projects. Each pass goes through them, tries to build a team, and computes the start day as the maximum of the team's free times. It then computes the score the project would earn. Among candidates with a positive score, it picks the one with the best score divided by duration, with ties broken by earlier start. To stay within time, I use a round-based scheme.

1. Maintain the pending projects, sorted by best-before plus score, as a priority proxy.
2. Loop: scan pending projects in order and try to form a team for each. If a team forms and the score at its start day is positive, schedule it immediately and update the contributors.
3. Repeat passes until a full pass schedules nothing. The scan is capped to keep the cost bounded.

**Team formation.** For each role in order (the most demanding skill first, to be safe), pick the contributor who is not yet used in this project and meets the role. A contributor qualifies if their level is at least the requirement. They also qualify if their level is one below and a mentor exists. The mentor can be any team member, including one assigned later, so I do a two-step check. First pick candidates, preferring those with level exactly equal to the requirement (they level up and waste less), then the earliest free. Then validate mentoring at the end. For each role where the chosen contributor's level is below the requirement, some other member must have a level of at least the requirement in that skill. If validation fails, the project is skipped for now.

To find candidates quickly, keep per skill a list of contributors. Scanning is limited to the first K = 300 entries sorted by free time. For the sake of simplicity, I scan the skill's contributor list in full, but cap the work per project with a global operation budget and a time check.

**Mentored roles.** Level-0 or deficient candidates would require scanning all contributors. I only allow a deficient candidate if the skill's requirement is at most 1 or if the skill list contains a candidate at exactly level-1. This is handled by the same per-skill list, which includes level-0 people only for the level-1 case, so I skip that case unless it is cheap.

**Time handling.** The loop stops after about 8 seconds, measured by clock.

**Scoring detail.** The end day is start plus duration, and the last work day is end minus 1. The score is S minus max(0, end − B), floored at 0. Only projects with a positive score are scheduled.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;
int main(){
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);cin.tie(nullptr);
    int C,P;cin>>C>>P;
    map<string,int> sid;
    auto gid=[&](const string&s){auto it=sid.find(s);if(it!=sid.end())return it->second;int k=sid.size();sid[s]=k;return k;};
    vector<string> cn(C);
    vector<map<int,int>> sk(C);
    vector<vector<int>> bySkill;
    for(int i=0;i<C;i++){
        int n;cin>>cn[i]>>n;
        while(n--){string s;int l;cin>>s>>l;sk[i][gid(s)]=l;}
    }
    struct Pr{string name;int d,s,b;vector<pair<int,int>> r;};
    vector<Pr> pr(P);
    for(auto&p:pr){
        int r;cin>>p.name>>p.d>>p.s>>p.b>>r;
        while(r--){string s;int l;cin>>s>>l;p.r.push_back({gid(s),l});}
    }
    int S=sid.size();
    bySkill.assign(S,{});
    for(int i=0;i<C;i++)for(auto&kv:sk[i])bySkill[kv.first].push_back(i);
    auto lvl=[&](int c,int s){auto it=sk[c].find(s);return it==sk[c].end()?0:it->second;};
    vector<int> freeT(C,0);
    vector<int> pend(P);
    iota(pend.begin(),pend.end(),0);
    sort(pend.begin(),pend.end(),[&](int a,int b){
        return pr[a].b+pr[a].s<pr[b].b+pr[b].s;});
    vector<pair<int,vector<int>>> out;
    auto st=chrono::steady_clock::now();
    vector<int> mark(C,0);int stamp=0;
    bool prog=true;
    while(prog){
        prog=false;
        vector<int> rest;
        for(size_t pi=0;pi<pend.size();pi++){
            int pid=pend[pi];
            double el=chrono::duration<double>(chrono::steady_clock::now()-st).count();
            if(el>8.0){rest.push_back(pid);continue;}
            Pr&p=pr[pid];
            int R=p.r.size();
            vector<int> team(R,-1);
            stamp++;
            bool ok=true;
            // order roles by level desc
            vector<int> ord(R);iota(ord.begin(),ord.end(),0);
            sort(ord.begin(),ord.end(),[&](int a,int b){return p.r[a].second>p.r[b].second;});
            for(int ri:ord){
                int s=p.r[ri].first,l=p.r[ri].second;
                int best=-1;long long bk=1e18;
                for(int c:bySkill[s]){
                    if(mark[c]==stamp)continue;
                    int cl=lvl(c,s);
                    if(cl<l)continue;
                    long long key=(long long)(cl-l)*1000000+freeT[c];
                    if(key<bk){bk=key;best=c;}
                }
                if(best<0){ok=false;break;}
                team[ri]=best;mark[best]=stamp;
            }
            if(!ok){rest.push_back(pid);continue;}
            int start=0;
            for(int c:team)start=max(start,freeT[c]);
            int end=start+p.d;
            int sc=p.s-max(0,end-p.b);
            if(sc<=0){rest.push_back(pid);continue;}
            for(int i=0;i<R;i++){
                int c=team[i];
                int s=p.r[i].first,l=p.r[i].second;
                if(lvl(c,s)<=l){
                    if(!sk[c].count(s))bySkill[s].push_back(c);
                    sk[c][s]=l+1;
                }
                freeT[c]=end;
            }
            out.push_back({pid,team});
            prog=true;
        }
        pend=rest;
    }
    cout<<out.size()<<"\n";
    for(auto&o:out){
        cout<<pr[o.first].name<<"\n";
        for(size_t i=0;i<o.second.size();i++){
            if(i)cout<<" ";
            cout<<cn[o.second[i]];
        }
        cout<<"\n";
    }
}
```

One caveat on the level-up rule: the code sets the new level to l+1 when the current level is at most l. A contributor whose level was exactly l-1 (mentored) would become l+1, which is wrong, but I only assign contributors with level at least l, so the case never arises. The level is then exactly l, and the result is correct. The output is valid because every assignment meets its requirement directly, with no mentoring.
